<Docs title="BeastUI" description="A calm, outlined UI system.">
  <h1>BeastUI</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">A calm, outlined UI system. Tokens, plain CSS classes, and thin Preact components.</p>

  <h2>Why I built it</h2>
  <p>
    I make my own apps: Masterz, an audio mastering tool, and Attaque Défense, a game played on phones. Each one
    grew its own buttons, tables and colours, and each one solved the same problems a little differently. BeastUI is
    the one system they all share, so a fix made once lands everywhere, and a new app starts finished instead of
    from scratch.
  </p>

  <h2>The intention</h2>
  <p>
    A product, not a components folder. BeastUI has its own repo, its own versions on npm and these docs. It is
    built in three layers so any app can take what it needs:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tokens</strong>: colours, type, spacing and lines as CSS custom properties, for any stack.</li>
    <li><strong>CSS</strong>: every component as plain classes that work with zero JavaScript.</li>
    <li><strong>Preact</strong>: thin wrappers that add behaviour, keyboard support and accessibility.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    An app that is not written in Preact still gets most of the system from the first two layers.
  </p>

  <h2>The style</h2>
  <p>
    Clear, abstract and outlined: paper and ink. Line and shape do the work, not heavy fills or shadows. Colour is
    rare, so it means something when it shows up. Motion is there only when it explains what happened.
  </p>
  <p>
    Calm by default, because a work tool needs to stay out of the way. An app can opt into a playful layer with
    more colour and life, and still looks like the same family.
  </p>

  <h2>The bar</h2>
  <p>
    Light and dark from day one, AA contrast, a visible focus ring, everything usable from the keyboard, and respect
    for reduced motion. A smaller system that is finished beats a big one that is not.
  </p>
</Docs>
